course: dictionary
lesson: 12319
title: Corrosion: slow damage
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Corrosion is slow damage. Water, air or chemicals gradually wear away metal or stone. The best-known example is iron turning to rust. You can often spot corrosion by looking for changes in colour, flaking or a rough, crumbly surface.
explain: «The old garden gate showed signs of corrosion after years in the rain.» The word «signs» tells you the damage built up slowly, over time, rather than happening all at once. Corrosion is a noun, so it names the damage itself.
explain: «Salt water causes corrosion, so the boats were painted every year.» The clue is in the second half: the boats were painted to protect them. Painting stops water and air reaching the metal, so you can guess that corrosion is something people try to prevent.
fact: Corrosion means the slow damage of metal or stone by water, air or chemicals.
fact: Rust is what iron becomes when it corrodes.
fact: Corrosion is a chemical change: iron reacts with water and oxygen to form rust.
fact: Salt water speeds up corrosion, which is why cars and piers by the sea corrode faster.
fact: People stop corrosion with paint, oil or grease, or by galvanising iron, which means coating it with zinc.
exercise: «The garden gate was covered in corrosion after years in the rain.» What does corrosion mean here? | *Slow damage by water and air | A bright shine from polishing | A sweet smell | A loud noise from the hinges
exercise: Which of these is an example of corrosion? | A ball bouncing on the grass | A clean window shining in the sun | Paint drying on a wall | *An iron nail turning to rust
exercise: Nadia says, «Corrosion is the same as cleaning.» Is she right? | No, corrosion is damage that happens slowly | Yes, both make metal shine | *No, corrosion is slow damage, the opposite of cleaning | Yes, both happen only in the rain
test: What does «protect against corrosion» mean? | *Keep something safe from slow damage | Sell something at the shop | Make something rust faster | Make something very hot
test: Why do cars by the sea corrode faster? | *Because salt water speeds up corrosion | Because the air is always dry | Because sea breezes are warm | Because the cars are new
exam: Which statement about corrosion is true? | *Rust forms when iron reacts with water and oxygen | Corrosion is a sudden, quick break | Corrosion only happens to wood | Salt water stops corrosion
